  2) AS-CORELINK *did* try to announce 44.192.100.0/24 directly to TransitAS (citing the AltDB record as "justification"). TransitAS rejected it as RPKI-invalid and formally warned CORELINK that another attempt = AUP violation.
  3) Tier1 has been notified to apply Route Origin Validation and drop the RPKI-invalid /24 they appear to have accepted from elsewhere.

So: on our side of the network (ISP and TinyInc) the routing is clean. The 3-hop "hijacked" path we observed is downstream of Tier1, outside our control, and being escalated. Traffic from you to real AWS prefixes (e.g. 10.255.1.1) is correctly using the legitimate /16 path. The Celer Bridge /24 remains compromised until Tier1 applies ROV — continue to treat 44.192.100.0/24 as untrusted.

Good-known reachable targets (unchanged): 85.12.64.1 (me), 5.62.56.1 (CORELINK's legit prefix), 10.255.2.1, 10.255.6.1, 10.255.1.1 (real AWS loopback).

I'll let you know if Tier1 resolves it.
